## Why This Is Asked

This tests practical understanding of lock-free IPC, memory ordering, and backpressure under contention.

## Key Concepts

- Lock-free ring buffer across producers and a consumer
- CAS-based enqueue and acquire/release semantics
- Backpressure strategies and fairness
- Testing under contention

## Code Example

```c
// Producer sketch
bool enqueue(ring_t *r, void *d){
  unsigned int pos = atomic_fetch_add(&r->tail, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
  if (pos - r->head >= r->size) return false;
  r->slots[pos % r->size] = d;
  atomic_thread_fence(memory_order_release);
  return true;
}
```

```c
// Consumer sketch
void *dequeue(ring_t *r){
  unsigned int h = atomic_load(&r->head, memory_order_acquire);
  unsigned int t = atomic_load(&r->tail, memory_order_acquire);
  if (h == t) return NULL;
  void *d = r->slots[h % r->size];
  atomic_store(&r->head, h+1);
  return d;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to multiple consumers without races?
- How does cache coherence affect performance with large ring sizes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical understanding of page fault handling, page cache coordination, IO paths, and race condition handling under contention.

## Key Concepts
- Page fault handling workflow: detection, frame allocation, IO vs zero-fill
- Per-page locking and CAS-based inflight signaling to serialize IO
- PTE updates, TLB invalidation, and waking waiters
- Throughput considerations under concurrent faults

## Code Example
```c
// Minimal sketch of per-page fault handling (pseudo-C)
typedef struct { int inflight; void* frame; int swapped; } Page;
void handle_fault(Page* p) {
  if (atomic_compare_and_swap(&p->inflight, 0, 1) == 0) {
    p->frame = allocate_frame();
    if (p->swapped) read_from_storage(p->frame);
    else zero_fill(p->frame);
    update_pte(p, p->frame);
    invalidate_tlb();
    wake_waiters(p);
    p->inflight = 0;
  } else {
    wait_for_completion(p);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test correctness with multiple concurrent faulting threads?
- What are potential race conditions besides inflight handling, and how would you mitigate them?

## Why This Is Asked

Beginners should reason about virtual memory protection, per-thread isolation, and fault handling without requiring full OS support. Guard pages are a simple, practical mechanism to detect overflows early.

## Key Concepts

- Virtual memory protection and per-thread stacks
- Guard page placement and page faults
- Graceful thread termination and resource cleanup
- Basic testing for concurrency and fault paths

## Code Example

```c
// Minimal skeleton
typedef struct { void *stack; void *guard; } Thread;

void thread_init(Thread *t) {
  t->stack = mmap(NULL, 8*1024*1024, PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE,
                  MAP_PRIVATE|MAP_ANONYMOUS, -1, 0);
  t->guard = (char*)t->stack - PAGE_SIZE;
  mprotect(t->guard, PAGE_SIZE, PROT_NONE);
}

void fault_handler(void *addr, Thread *t) {
  if (addr >= t->guard && addr < (char*)t->guard + PAGE_SIZE) {
    terminate_thread(t);
  }
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to optionally grow the stack on demand?
- What testing strategy ensures no collateral damage when multiple threads fault simultaneously?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of race conditions, safe resource reuse, and lightweight OS primitives in a beginner-friendly context.

## Key Concepts

- Bitmaps for ID allocation
- Generation counters to distinguish reuse
- Simple locking strategy to avoid races
- Handling exhaustion and graceful error paths

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
  uint16_t id;      // 0..65535
  uint16_t gen;     // generation counter
  bool in_use;
} pid_slot;

// Global state (simplified)
pid_slot table[65536];
pthread_mutex_t pid_lock;

int alloc_pid(void) {
  pthread_mutex_lock(&pid_lock);
  for (uint32_t i = 1; i < 65536; i++) {
    if (!table[i].in_use) {
      table[i].in_use = true;
      table[i].gen++;
      int pid = (table[i].gen << 16) | i;
      pthread_mutex_unlock(&pid_lock);
      return pid;
    }
  }
  pthread_mutex_unlock(&pid_lock);
  return -1; // exhausted
}

void release_pid(uint32_t pid) {
  uint16_t idx = pid & 0xFFFF;
  pthread_mutex_lock(&pid_lock);
  table[idx].in_use = false;
  pthread_mutex_unlock(&pid_lock);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you implement a non-blocking alloc_pid() without a global lock?
- How to handle PID namespace isolation across containers?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of fine-grained synchronization, deadlock avoidance strategies, and kernel-space data structures for I/O-heavy workloads. It demonstrates a candidate's ability to balance correctness with high concurrency in performance-critical systems.

## Key Concepts
- **Interval Trees**: Efficient data structures for storing and querying overlapping ranges, supporting O(log n) insertion and lookup
- **Range Locks**: Fine-grained locking allowing concurrent writes to non-overlapping regions of memory-mapped files
- **Deadlock Avoidance**: Global lock ordering, lock escalation, or hierarchical locking to prevent circular wait conditions
- **Concurrency Primitives**: CAS operations for lock-free checks, futex-like wait queues for efficient blocking/wakeup, and careful handling of reentrancy

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct RangeLock {
    int64_t start;
    int64_t end;
    atomic_t refcnt;
    struct RangeLock *left;
    struct RangeLock *right;
    wait_queue_head_t waitq;
} RangeLock;

RangeLock* acquire_range(int64_t start, int64_t end) {
    RangeLock *parent = NULL, *node = root;

    while (node) {
        if (ranges_overlap(node->start, node->end, start, end)) {
            wait_event(node->waitq, atomic_read(&node->refcnt) == 0);
            continue;
        }
        parent = node;
        node = (start < node->start) ? node->left : node->right;
    }

    RangeLock *new = kmalloc(sizeof(RangeLock));
    new->start = start; new->end = end;
    atomic_set(&new->refcnt, 1);
    init_waitqueue_head(&new->waitq);

    if (!parent) root = new;
    else if (start < parent->start) parent->left = new;
    else parent->right = new;

    return new;
}

void release_range(RangeLock *node) {
    atomic_set(&node->refcnt, 0);
    wake_up_all(&node->waitq);
}

int downgrade_range(RangeLock *node) {
    return atomic_dec_return(&node->refcnt);
}
```

## Key Design Considerations
- **Global Ordering**: Always acquire locks in consistent address order to prevent deadlocks
- **Wakeup Semantics**: FIFO wakeup ordering prevents starvation
- **Lock Escalation**: Coalesce fine-grained locks under high contention to reduce overhead
- **Memory Efficiency**: Pre-allocate node pools to avoid allocation in the fast path

